cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:11:# Kaggriculture BC trainer warm-starts PPO from the held-out best
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:13:Plan Task 5.2 asks for a BC trainer on the rebuilt model that reuses Isaiah's optimizer factory, BF16 and compile path, keeps the best checkpoint by held-out NLL and stops on sustained held-out degradation (lesson L9). This change builds the trainer script and its tests. The BC run itself waits for the Task 5.1 shards and the pod.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:17:- `scripts/train_bc.py`: CLI in `run_ppo.py`'s shape (fresh `CONFIG --data --output-dir`, restart from a run directory, `torchrun` for several GPUs, `--log-mode`, `--wandb-mode online|offline`, `--max-runtime-hours`, `--source-commit` on a fresh launch or a restart). Each launch or restart appends an attempt record to `bc_attempts.jsonl` (see "Restart identity" below). It runs the GEMM workload check for the BC microbatch and validation forwards and the compile-stack check. It then builds the model with `create_model` and `reset_parameters`, compiles through `configure_model_compile` (the Kaggriculture cuBLAS-only claim), builds the optimizer with `create_optimizer` / `create_lr_scheduler` and wraps it with Isaiah's DDP adapter.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:18:- `python/owl/train/bc.py`: `BCConfig`, the objective, `TrainSharding`, `HeldOutSelection`, checkpoint and resume state, a W&B logger (project `kg-v3`, `job_type` `bc`) and `train_bc`. BF16 comes from the PPO config's `rl.dtype` through `autocast_context`. The model, dtype and compile settings come from the PPO config that BC warm-starts (`ppo_config`), so the BC checkpoint matches that PPO run's model. `wrap_bc_model_for_distributed` casts the model for Isaiah's Orbit-annotated DDP adapter, whose dispatch passes Kaggriculture batches through unchanged. The Task 3.1 trainer seam has not generalized those annotations yet.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:19:- `python/owl/kaggriculture/bc_data.py`: the shard writer and loader. `configs/bc/kaggriculture_2rank.yaml`: the 2-rank BC config for `configs/kaggriculture_2rank.yaml`. It sits under `configs/bc/` because the existing config tests load every top-level `configs/*.yaml` as a PPO `FullConfig`. `README.md` has a new "Kaggriculture BC warm start" section.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:23:- **The critic is trained, not frozen.** Isaiah trains all of the shared trunk and both heads together, and his teacher distillation weights policy KL and winner CE equally (`teacher_kl_coef = teacher_value_coef`). BC is distillation from the recorded player. So the loss is the turn NLL divided by program length, plus `value_coef` (1.0) times the per-seat winner CE against the episode's raw final banks (L1: the larger bank wins, a draw is 0.5 each). The reference froze the critic head, but its trunk still moved, so its critic reached PPO miscalibrated with no target. A test shows that a policy-only loss leaves the four `critic_head` tensors without gradients. Isaiah's adapter builds DDP without unused-parameter detection for this model, so the joint loss also gives every parameter a gradient. DDP itself was not run. Isaiah's own recipe used no imitation learning (`docs/write-up.md`); BC here is the plan's owner-required addition.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:24:- **Shard format.** The loader follows the refreshed 5.1 brief (branch `kg/rebuild-bc-brief` at `bcefd02`, still awaiting Codex review). Shards are `kaggriculture-bc-shard-v1`: one `np.savez_compressed` per episode at `<split>/<day>-<episode_id>.npz`, with the 28 observation fields, `can_act`, `tokens`, `lengths`, `turn` and a `schema` id. The brief lists the manifest's contents but not its key names. The loader requires `schema`, `observation_schema_version`, `contract_document_version` and per-episode `episode_id`, `split`, `terminal_banks`, `admitted`, `shard_path`, `shard_sha256` and `shard_bytes`. It keeps other custody keys uninterpreted. Task 5.1's `prepare.py` must emit these names, or use `write_bc_episode` / `write_bc_manifest`.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:25:- **Residency and sharding.** Each rank keeps rows `[rank::world_size]` of every episode in host memory (the reference's split). Exact integers are stored as range-checked int32 and tokens as int16, then gathered back as int64. Loading verifies every shard's SHA-256, byte count and schema id, and runs the full `check_contract` once. Training order is a permutation seeded by `(seed, epoch, rank)` over that rank's rows, so restarting from `bc_state.pt` under the same source and settings repeats the uninterrupted run's rows, updates, evaluation steps and stopping step on a deterministic device (the kept checkpoint can differ; see "Selection and stopping"). Every evaluation covers all validation rows. A preflight pass admits every training program through `evaluate_actions` before step 1.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:26:- **Selection and stopping.** Each evaluation's held-out NLL feeds `HeldOutSelection`, which keeps selection and patience apart. Every strict new minimum is written as `checkpoint_bc_best.pt`, with a SHA-256 record in `checkpoint_bc_best.json`. Patience counts evaluations since the last improvement of more than `min_delta` over the previous such improvement, so gains inside `min_delta` still replace the best checkpoint but do not reset the count (the early-stopping convention; the first version gated the checkpoint by `min_delta` too, which Codex verification r1 showed could discard the lowest NLL). Training stops when that count reaches `patience_evals`, at `max_steps` or at the runtime cap. Patience counts scheduled evaluations only. A budget or runtime stop between them evaluates the final weights once (`bc/scheduled_evaluation` 0 in `bc_history.jsonl`); that evaluation can replace the best checkpoint but leaves the patience count alone. Codex verification r2 showed that counting it let a run stopped at step 1 and extended to 4 stop at step 2, where the uninterrupted run stopped at step 4. With it excluded, the resumed run evaluates and stops at the uninterrupted steps, and its best checkpoint is the minimum over a superset of the same weights, so never higher in held-out NLL. This is a choice made here: Isaiah has no BC loop and PPO has no held-out patience rule.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:27:- **Restart identity.** A restart is a new attempt, not a continuation of the launch's identity. `bc_attempts.jsonl` gets one record per launch or restart: attempt index, that checkout's source (`git rev-parse HEAD` with a `-dirty` suffix, or `--source-commit`), the lineage of every attempt's source, start step, the parent `bc_state.pt` SHA-256, dataset root and manifest hash, world size and start time. The best-checkpoint record and `bc_result.json` copy the attempt that wrote them. `bc_state.pt` stores the trajectory settings (the BC config except `max_steps`, and the whole PPO config by content); a restart with any other difference fails and names the changed fields, so only the step budget can grow. A changed source is recorded, not rejected: exact replay is claimed only for an unchanged source. `bc_history.jsonl` holds the NLL curve and `bc_result.json` the stopping step and reason. The best checkpoint has exactly `run_ppo.py`'s keys with `env_steps` 0, next to the PPO `config.yaml`.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:32:- `tests/kaggriculture/test_bc.py` (30 tests) uses tiny CPU models and synthetic shards whose programs a second tiny model sampled. The tests cover:
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:33:  - the shard round trip at contract dtypes, and rejection of a tampered hash, a duplicate episode, an old `features/context` shard, a missing split, unpaired rows and out-of-range compaction;
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:35:  - a hand-computed objective oracle (per-frame probabilities, lengths and winner probabilities in, the length-normalized NLL plus CE out), and `train_bc`'s own update path moving every floating tensor and lowering the full training objective by more than 10%;
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:41:  - the 2-rank BC config targeting the 2-rank PPO config;
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:49:- Codex's other reported wiring gaps remain untested on CPU: removing autocast, compile, DDP, startup workload/stack checks, accumulation normalization, clipping, scheduler stepping, preflight or the runtime cap survives the suite, and several malformed shard/config guards have no negative fixture (the attempt-record guards now do).
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:50:- Nothing has run on a GPU yet: no BF16 autocast, compiled trunk, DDP/NCCL, pinned-memory transfer or live W&B. The owner's Mac ran FP32 with no compilation. Throughput, peak memory and load time are unmeasured: each rank decompresses every shard, gathers on the host each step, and `clip_grad_norm_(error_if_nonfinite=True)` syncs once per step. Host memory per rank for the real slice is unmeasured too.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-bc-trainer-warm-starts-ppo-from-the-held-out-best.md:51:- The BC run needs the 5.1 shards. Their token half waits on the Task 1.4 `kaggriculture_encode/decode` binding, and the full-slice run needs a machine that can read volume `4llk4uaf20`. It also needs the pod and FlashAttention, because `configs/model/kaggriculture.yaml` forces it (plan 6.0). PPO from the BC best then needs `run_ppo.py` to run Kaggriculture (Tasks 1.4/3.1). Today only its loaders are exercised.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:4:description: "Task 3.1 model side: KaggricultureTransformerConfig is in the shared ModelConfig union and create_model with game-checked specs, the trunk compile target dispatches through a nominal TrunkCompileAPI that compiles only the blocks behind _run_trunk's overflow guard, and the winner softmax uses Isaiah's masked form; CPU tests only; FullConfig now pairs it with the Task 3.4 KaggricultureEnvConfig."
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:20:  - `python/owl/train/config.py`: this change made the validator reject `kaggriculture_transformer`, because `FullConfig.env` was Orbit's `EnvConfig`. Task 3.4's second fix replaced that rejection with the env pairing ([[kaggriculture-configs-follow-isaiahs-scaling-6m-recipe|configs Reference]]); the validator still never reads Orbit-only fields (`actor`, `value_mode`, `critic_mode`) from the Kaggriculture config.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:22:  - `TrunkCompileAPI` (`python/owl/model/base.py`) is a nominal ABC with `compile_transformer_trunk(*, mode) -> int`. `StatelessTransformerV1` and `KaggricultureTransformer` both implement it.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:26:- **Critic.** The Task 2.2 brief asked for a masked winner softmax, and Isaiah uses `masked_softmax(logits, still_playing)`. The code used a plain `log_softmax`.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:27:  - `_winner_log_probabilities` now follows Isaiah's `_critic_distillation`. It fills masked logits with the dtype minimum, then applies `log_softmax`.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic.md:40:  - `FullConfig` rejects the model without a Kaggriculture env (the original test asserted rejection under Orbit's `EnvConfig`; its message still matches the pairing error)
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection.md:71:- The prefix embeddings (std `D^-0.5`) are added to a LayerNorm-scaled base with no second norm, as the brief specifies. At initialization the prefix signal is about `1/sqrt(D)` of the base. Whether this conditions strongly enough is untested; reopen if per-slot KL or BC accuracy shows weak item/quantity conditioning on kind.
cookbook/references/kaggriculture-encoder-reuses-isaiah-stateless-layers.md:46:Task 2.2 adds Isaiah's critic: an `OutputProjectionMLP` over the two critic-value tokens, a winner softmax (masked as Isaiah's since Task 3.1), value `2p(self) − 1`, output gain 1.0 excluded from Muon. Task 2.3's action heads and the full-model budget (6,252,223 parameters) are in the [[kaggriculture-grammar-heads-sit-behind-isaiahs-actor-projection|heads Reference]]. Shared factory registration, the trunk compile dispatch and the masked critic are in the [[kaggriculture-model-joins-isaiahs-factory-compile-and-masked-critic|Task 3.1 model-side Reference]]; trainer integration is open. `check_contract` checks dtypes, shapes and bounds, not the semantic zero-fill and privacy rules; those are asserted only for the synthetic fixture, and the native writer (Task 1.x) must be tested against them separately.

